{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres","id":1124237,"title":"Actual Science implications with the Spheres","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-03T03:24:33-07:00","id":1124237,"title":"Actual Science implications with the Spheres","content":"Hi, as a preamble, please accept my apologies if this post substantially duplicates anything that has been previously asked on this forum. <br /> <br /> I have two remarks about Science as it relates to the Spheres. (As an aside, IRL I am an academic in the field of Biochem. So, haha, probably part of the Technocracy.)<br /> <br /> Life Sphere: I'm a bit unclear on what criteria the developers were using to delineate between the categories &quot;Simple&quot; and &quot;Complex&quot; with regards to living things.<br /> <br /> In M20, pp.516-517 &quot;Simple organisms \u2013 viruses, mollusks, insects, plants, etc..he can cause flowers to bloom or wither, help trees bear fruit, and so on&quot; and &quot;..on any complex organism \u2013 people, dogs, horses, and so forth.&quot; It appears, from reading this, that the authors' definition of complex is &quot;mammal&quot;...or possibly &quot;vertebrate&quot;. The thing is, in terms of cellular architecture, any multicellular eukaryote (e.g. a plant, a mollusk (sic), arthropod) is just as evolved as a human. Sure, they may not be sentient in the same way we are, but I imagine that that particular trait would be covered by the Sphere of Mind. I accept that viruses, bacteria, protists and other <i>extremely </i>simple multicellulars (e.g. <i>Porifera</i>) can be covered by Rank 2. I'd argue that the rest of the multicellulars would be covered by the higher ranks (3+) of Life. Thoughts? <br /> <br /> What's the non-metagame justification for a player not using Life 3 to change ever present bacteria into a strain of MRSA? Or rhinovirus (not in a human <i>yet</i>, as that would require a higher rank) into Ebola?<br /> <br /> Other Spheres: It seems that &quot;Science&quot; itself, doesn't work when lined up against the Spheres. The demarcated 9 areas of magical influence are odd, when considered under the lens of Science. This might seem a pointless query to some, but I think it might be pertinent - particularly if trying to understand things from antagonist/protagonist Technocrat points of view. Consider Entropy: according to Thermodynamics, Entropy and Energy are tied together. We can talk about their relationship through Gibbs' Free Energy and the Laws of Thermodynamics. Does a Technocrat character understand the Spheres as written (as implied by &quot;Dimensional Science&quot;/&quot;Primal Utility&quot;/&quot;Data&quot;) or is it better to explain things under the auspices of Science (i.e. Physics, Chemistry etc)? If the latter is true, how does it line up?<br /> <br /> I'm keen to know what others' thoughts are.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13778-cerulean-scream","name":"Cerulean Scream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13778_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T05:36:19-07:00","id":1124260,"title":"","content":"There are a number of other places to draw the line between simple and complex life. Here are two: One of the distinctions in multicellular life is the difference between a diploblast and a triploblast. The actual definition of the two is actually based on specific stages of embryonic development, but the lay explanation is that triploblasts have a complex internal structure; a third place cells can be (inside, outside, and within). There is an argument in the biology community that the vertebrate neural crest counts as a fourth 'layer' and that vertebrates count as quadroblasts.<br /> <br /> As far as the various factions understandings, the Spheres (in game) are more of a way of describing a Mage's capabilities rather than a way of understanding the world. Technocracts will almost certainly think in terms of scientific principles first and 'spheres' only in their after action reports.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T06:35:34-07:00","id":1124277,"title":"","content":"OK, great, I agree with your raising of the issue of tissue differentiation (i.e. mesoderm, ectoderm etc) as a way to distinguish between complex and simple animals, but it makes things even more stringent for Life 2, particularly as any animal more complex than a flatworm is triploblastic.<br /> <br /> Also, it still doesn't necessarily explain why insects are considered &quot;simple&quot;. Would you disallow <i>actual</i> complex animals (triploblasts e.g. flatworms) by Life 2...or would you use some fuzzy logic to explain Life 2 effects such as &quot;giving a crab wings&quot;?<br /> <br /> Thanks for your thoughts on Technocracy/Spheres, Ramnesis. I think we're generally on the same page.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13778-cerulean-scream","name":"Cerulean Scream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13778_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T07:36:44-07:00","id":1124293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerulean Scream</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres?p=1124277#post1124277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, great, I agree with your raising of the issue of tissue differentiation (i.e. mesoderm, ectoderm etc) as a way to distinguish between complex and simple animals, but it makes things even more stringent for Life 2, particularly as any animal more complex than a flatworm is triploblastic.<br /> <br /> Also, it still doesn't necessarily explain why insects are considered &quot;simple&quot;. Would you disallow <i>actual</i> complex animals (triploblasts e.g. flatworms) by Life 2...or would you use some fuzzy logic to explain Life 2 effects such as &quot;giving a crab wings&quot;?<br /> <br /> Thanks for your thoughts on Technocracy/Spheres, Ramnesis. I think we're generally on the same page.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Given your background, I'd actually like to ask how Hall's Quadroblast idea is regarded. From what I can tell it isn't widely accepted (or derided), which makes it hard to use as an actual distinction for our purposes. On the plus side it is expedient. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I don't like making Spheres do different things for different people. Out of game it defeats the purpose of having a universal magic system, and in game it defeats the purpose of having an agreed upon terminology. So I wouldn't disallow a scientific paradigm from manipulating crabs, and I would use fuzzy logic if I couldn't explain why. That said, I prefer to find a reasonable balance between the scientific and folkloric justifications.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T07:47:34-07:00","id":1124297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres?p=1124293#post1124293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Given your background, I'd actually like to ask how Hall's Quadroblast idea is regarded. From what I can tell it isn't widely accepted (or derided), which makes it hard to use as an actual distinction for our purposes. On the plus side it is expedient. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I can't say that I'm au fait with Hall's work beyond the suggestion of the neural crest being a separate layer - that's more the realm of an evolutionary developmental biologist. My area is more to do with smaller things - macromolecules and so on. I will, however, ask my neurochem colleagues tomorrow, and ask them their opinion and then message you.<br /> <br /> Thanks for your thoughts on Spheres/Science","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13778-cerulean-scream","name":"Cerulean Scream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13778_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T11:41:49-07:00","id":1124410,"title":"","content":"Keep in mind that in Mage, the authors are trying to explain what you can do in a world where people have many different views of life. The metaphors don't always work well.<br /> <br /> Eukaryotes are more complex than prokaryotes, but I think the big reason Life 2 and Life 3 divide on complex organisms is so that your mage can't study just a little Life and start killing humans with Life magic or creating life. Forces in Mage 1e had a similarly strange split--gravity and nuclear forces required Forces 4 but you could do light and sound and make your mage invisible with Forces 2 (usually had to through in another sphere instead of flat Forces 2).<br /> <br /> The Spheres are thus an in-game codification for gameplay, but every mage is going to see them operating differently. Ontogeny does not recapitulate phylogeny (Hackel's Law), but we do know you can see a lot of cool things about how life works from studying development. The same is true for physics and chemistry--some things that look really simple are in fact complex processes which depend on time and location to work out a certain way.<br /> <br /> As for science, not every player of Mage will have a science degree, not every player of Mage will have paid attention in pre-college science courses and I imagine some players have religious views that make them disbelief some current scientific trends. So, ultimately, since our modern scientific method is a paradigm, not everyone will be applying this. Think of your fellow players and the Storyteller when going too sciency on them.<br /> <br /> I remember trying to explain the Lotka-Volterra predator prey model in a history course to explain how one particular group of First Peoples (Canadian term for Native Americans) were the worst impacted by the British Empire. My explanation went over everyone's head and there were some pretty smart people in the room. They just didn't think about ecology in that way. . .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T12:56:12-07:00","id":1124445,"title":"","content":"So Mary is a Physicist, and Anne is a Mystic. Mary knows that the Earth is flying through space at high speed. Anne rejected education at a young age and thus has no conception of the Earth moving.<br /> <br /> Mary tries to travel forwards in time, 3 hours, using her prototype Time Machine. She succeeds, and appears in the same spot (having no Correspondence). She knows that she isn't in the same &quot;place&quot; in the universe, but she isn't going to appear in the midst of space with the earth having left her behind in its flight. She can explain this with technobabble; my calculations adjusted for it, the time machine's pattern is tethered to the equivalent location by a magnetic resonance field, yada yada.<br /> <br /> Anne has nothing to explain away. Of COURSE she lands in the same place.<br /> <br /> So basically we have the same rules being applied to people with vastly different conceptions of reality. Mary might reasonably appear mid-space if she Botched very badly, but we can almost always glaze over the scientific issues with time travel. This applies to every Sphere. Turning Carbon into a Diamond is the same Sphere effect for everyone; Anne has NO idea that Diamonds are Carbon (unless she has investigated with Matter 1), while Mary can get a Coincidental effect going here; this machine crushes Carbon into a Diamond, it's a prototype, but everyone can believe this working. Despite Anne thinking she is performing a grand transformation (from worthless coal to a perfect gem, a huge distinction in her paradigm), and Mary thinking her change is the SIMPLEST physical change in a chemical sense, they both have the same mechanics for the effect. This is a good thing, IMO; Paradigm should influence Coincidence and be effected by Reality Zones, but certain things shouldn't be much harder in some paradigms than others. If Mary thinks that these &quot;simple&quot; organisms are actually quite complicated, she can explain the gap by thinking about psychodynamic or quantum protections that &quot;higher&quot; organisms might have an advantage in. The crab isn't willing itself to stay the same shape, while the Sleeper is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T16:00:40-07:00","id":1124513,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerulean Scream</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres#post1124237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's the non-metagame justification for a player not using Life 3 to change ever present bacteria into a strain of MRSA? Or rhinovirus (not in a human <i>yet</i>, as that would require a higher rank) into Ebola?</div> </div> </div> </div>Aside from the moral and pragmatic reluctance a character might have to subject a person to such a disease (and risk causing an outbreak of infections)?<br /> <br /> Well first, not everyone with Life knows what those organisms are, aside from in passing. The layman doesn't know how Ebola works. Many don't even know what it <i>does</i>, other than being vaguely deadly. (See also that one scene from The Simpsons where a peasant woman calls out in the streets that she's dying of Cholera, despite the fact an actual sufferer wouldn't be anywhere but in a bed, literally shitting themselves to death). If you don't know what you're trying to create, how can you create it?<br /> <br /> This is why M20 presents the optional rule for Minimum Abilities, reflecting a need for a base understanding of a subject before you can use it to do magick. In our example, a character needs a number of dots in Science before they can start turning lesser microorganisms into deadly ones.<br /> <br /> Further, all that assumes a character could make MRSA or Ebola at all, even if they were familiar with them from a biological standpoint. Not every Mage can. Their Focus limits the practical extent of their abilities. Even if, say, a Dreamspeaker believed that single celled organisms and viruses existed, and could cause disease, they wouldn't necessarily be able to work with them. A Shaman's training is based on practices thousands of years old, working on a theoretical framework for reality that doesn't line up with Science. What spirits does he contact to turn these tiny creatures into different, deadly ones? How does he even begin to understand what will make those spirits cooperative enough to do it for him?<br /> <br /> Same with a Hermetic wizard. Which pentacle does she draw to channel MRSA? Which planet and zodiac signs govern Ebola? What words in Enochian correspond to them? What materials have sympathetic relevance that can be exploited to work with the diseases? Is Ebola higher or lower than the Common Cold on the medieval hierarchy of being?<br /> <br /> Even if they personally believe the microorganisms exist, neither of these characters will necessarily have the tools to work with them. It's outside their mystical training, because tiny organisms that live in the bloodstream are a relatively recent Paradigmatic idea. At best, Life users from these Traditions would be working with what they know to afflict targets with symptoms similar to MRSA or Ebola, but which may or may not be them if a blood test is done after the fact. The Shaman contacts spirits of illness, the Hermetic tries to screw around with the target's humour balance, and neither could work extensively with the organisms outside the context of them being in a human, afflicting that human.<br /> <br /> Which doesn't mean a mystic mage had to stay that way. Mages haven't been idle in the centuries up to this point. They take the changes in the Consensus and try to adapt to the shifting &quot;meta&quot;. They experiment, do research, make educated guesses, cross-educate themselves on the subject, and talk to new spirits that arise in response to new developments. But a traditionally trained mystic is going to be lost, until they make those breakthroughs. And some don't want to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T19:09:04-07:00","id":1124570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres?p=1124513#post1124513\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aside from the moral and pragmatic reluctance a character might have to subject a person to such a disease (and risk causing an outbreak of infections)?</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with you. The moral quandaries associated of a regular, everyday person suddenly Awakening and being able to access reality-shaping powers are what makes MtA such an enjoyable world. I should clarify, though, as you've asked this. In my initial post, when I referred to non-metagame justifications, I meant in terms of the mechanics of the game system. I'm less asking about how paradigms view the differences in life (although I <i>did </i>ask exactly this with reference to Technocrats!), and more asking about how the developers delineate between complex and simple Life.. I'm sorry I didn't articulate myself properly. I'll try to do so below. I think the problem is that I tried to raise too many questions at once!<br /> <br /> Regardless, I appreciate the posts above explaining about how the Spheres are viewed through particular paradigms, as well as Bluecho's remarks about Minimum Abilities and Foci/Practices to make particular effects occur. My issue is less with the subjective viewpoint of the Awakened/Enlightened (thought it's nonetheless a useful reminder - I particularly like the bit about Hermetics and pentacles, as well as the time machine example given by 11twiggens) but more with how the in-game metaphysics intermesh with the rules set.<br /> <br /> As an aside, my games aren't science-laden - we're all there to have fun, not get bogged down with pedantry. I raised this question as a way of clearly understanding firstly how we, as players/STs, interpret the RAW with regards to complex and simple life. Whilst the WoD (in Mage, anyway) may have many competing realities, as STs we do need to have some boundaries in order to make rules decisions when questions arise. As my own games tend to be more cinematic, I like to have a clear understanding so my group and I can come to an agreed viewpoint on a rules issue, and move on, rather than having play halted with a long discussion. I accept that a non-Science mystic may not believe in/know about e.g. predator-prey theory/organic chemistry/viruses/relativity and so on, and that, in terms of their paradigm, this is unimportant with regards to making the magic effect happen. <br /> <br /> So my questions now are these:<br /> <br /> (1) Is there a firm metaphysical model that the various subjective realities are sitting on, or no? That is are the separations between Complex and Simple Life (or other examples) hard-coded into a sort of Ur-Reality that acts as a limiter on all paradigms? I'm really interested in what other people's views are on the metaphysics of Mage. (Separate question - Are there actually Spheres, or are they just examples of the further codification of reality? That is, did the usage of terms like &quot;Sphere&quot; cause magic to become more calcified/structured?) What do people do in their games? Personally, I take the approach that each Awakened's perception of reality is True (capitalised as I'm referring to Truth in the epistemological sense.)<br /> \u200b<br /> (2) How do you deal, as STs, with making the rules-call as to what constitutes &quot;complex&quot; and &quot;simple&quot;...or other more extreme effects (e.g. egregious uses of transmute Forces and/or Matter)? What do you think the developers intended? (I really liked the 11twiggen's argument about <i>Willpower</i> being key to maintaining morphic field, although it does suggest that force-of-will is a trait separate to Avatar, Mind etc - which isfine!)<br /> <br /> I appreciate all the guidance so far offered by others in this thread. It's extremely welcome. One of the great things about this world-set is that it can stimulate these sorts of discussions about the in-game nature of reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13778-cerulean-scream","name":"Cerulean Scream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13778_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-03T22:20:51-07:00","id":1124622,"title":"","content":"1. Spheres are a game mechanic to reflect that someone just starting to manipulate an aspect of reality can't transform things that someone who's really studied that aspect. This does calcify reality somewhat. If you really wanted to change things about a little, well I've been in a Vampire game where the first rank of Protean was claws instead of the vision and that's not necessarily game breaking. Although the spheres and the ranks are imperfect, they are good guidelines for what a person who can bend reality can and cannot do. If you have only the simplest understanding of how minds work, you should not be able to affect minds with magick because you cannot conceive of how to change someone's mind.<br /> <br /> Minimum abilities does add a layer of complexity to the game, but it means that characters who are really good at things can channel that will. It's something I like but would probably be loose on --if you can't really picture what a car looks like it will be much harder to create a working one. If you don't get the shamanic rites of Pacific Northwest First Peoples, then it's going to be hard doing something the way they do it. That being said, there are many examples of folk taxonomy working as well as more scientific taxonomy such as cladistics and Linneanism. I might define a bird differently than a Linnean by taking a cladistic perspective (to me it's part of the dinosaur line) and from one of the persons interviewed by UC Berkeley anthropologists in the early twentieth century, but we'd probably agree on certain common features and see connections to other life. I like the idea that mages are erudite, even if they know things I don't know.<br /> <br /> I deal with the fact that each mage's paradigm is not Truth by seeing Paradox as the universe's balance of counterveiling wills.<br /> <br /> 2. I don't know what each group of developers interpreted. Mage started as a game which the authors made clear had some connections to Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance. I have degrees in accounting, business, biology and history, so I tend to see reality as having both objective and subjective elements. While we can argue about the subjective elements and objectivity, in the end, those discussions are not very fruitful except for getting us to think. In a game, as in life, you have to make decisions with the information you have, the beliefs you have and with some idea but not a complete ideas of potential outcomes. So for Mage, I go with the rules that have been playtested and written and try and be fair. The tone of the game matters a lot. If you've gone with high adventure, mages should be able to get away with more spectacular effects than if you make it so that most magick is vulgar and mages are in constant fear of paradox.<br /> <br /> Two of Mage's greatest strengths, its flexible spellcasting system and its definition of reality, are also its greatest weaknesses.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-04T09:46:23-07:00","id":1124812,"title":"","content":"This has come up before in some of my own threads about Entropy, a sphere which, in my own games, I simply do not use. Mechanically, every single effect it makes is a duplication of other spheres. I don't like having a special UR-sphere that is OP.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/390220-is-the-sphere-of-entropy-redundant\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/390220-is-the-sphere-of-entropy-redundant\"> Is the sphere of Entropy redundant? - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/390220-is-the-sphere-of-entropy-redundant\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/390220-is-the-sphere-of-entropy-redundant </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> As the topic says, is the sphere of entropy actually needed in the game? Does it just step on the toes of other spheres? Specifically what can Entropy do is not </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-06T01:16:42-07:00","id":1125514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cerulean Scream</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1124237-actual-science-implications-with-the-spheres?p=1124570#post1124570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(1) Is there a firm metaphysical model that the various subjective realities are sitting on, or no? That is are the separations between Complex and Simple Life (or other examples) hard-coded into a sort of Ur-Reality that acts as a limiter on all paradigms? I'm really interested in what other people's views are on the metaphysics of Mage. (Separate question - Are there actually Spheres, or are they just examples of the further codification of reality? That is, did the usage of terms like &quot;Sphere&quot; cause magic to become more calcified/structured?) What do people do in their games? Personally, I take the approach that each Awakened's perception of reality is True (capitalised as I'm referring to Truth in the epistemological sense.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope, I wouldn't say so. There's some indication that some of the metaphysics that the <i>Spheres</i> - which are ultimately a Hermetic codification of how 'complex' a given 'spell' is, frogmarched into place by the Traditions Council so people with wildly different and incompatible understandings of reality can communicate without murdering each other (much) - are not necessarily constant over time. How big high-end Forces effects are in the Renaissance versus modern times, how 'permissive' time is, etc. The invention of bacteria and other microbes most likely transformed how easy it was to do Life effects for those 'in the know'.<br /> <br /> And then there's the metaphysics of <i>reality itself</i> as divorced from Consensus, which are very wibbly-wobbly from a human perspective and may or may not even exist at all.<br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (2) How do you deal, as STs, with making the rules-call as to what constitutes &quot;complex&quot; and &quot;simple&quot;...or other more extreme effects (e.g. egregious uses of transmute Forces and/or Matter)? What do you think the developers intended? (I really liked the 11twiggen's argument about <i>Willpower</i> being key to maintaining morphic field, although it does suggest that force-of-will is a trait separate to Avatar, Mind etc - which isfine!)<br /> <br /> I appreciate all the guidance so far offered by others in this thread. It's extremely welcome. One of the great things about this world-set is that it can stimulate these sorts of discussions about the in-game nature of reality. </div> </div> </div>For Life, I usually just use the vertebrate/invertebrate distinction for game-mechanics simplicity reasons and leave it at that, though I also am willing to work with a player who wants to fiddle around with Sphere effects to better match their character's paradigm. Things like turning into an animal earlier but personal enhancement later, for instance. If they have a particular reason to draw a different complex/simple organism boundary, well, they probably know more about the paradigm in particular as applicable to Life than I do and if it's not too egregiously abusable, I'd probably let 'em.<br /> <br /> If someone comes up with a clever application of transmuting Forces/Matter, say, and it becomes <i>too</i> problematic... well, that depends on what it is they're doing. Forces in particular requires level 3 to transmute, so that gives a bit of leeway, and anything with a <i>large</i> amount of energy or over a large area is going to require Forces 4-5 because that's what those levels denote. Blatant Matter transformations in many paradigms require a bit of downtime to pull off, either as part of the effect or as a 'create reagents' type thing (though I'm more thinking Hermetic and Technocratic).<br /> <br /> hey of course need to have the requisite knowledge as appropriate to their paradigm. You can assign the number of successes they need such that it can become onerous to just do something on the fly. But if it's a clever application of the Spheres in question that does in fact fit their paradigm, and the character has good reason to know what they're doing and has everything at hand to take their capabilities and wield them out of proportion to what one might superficially think a mere mortal to be capable of... well, good, they're being a Mage.<br /> <br /> The thing to keep in mind that even a Sphere 1 level, let alone a Sphere 2 level, can be devastatingly effective in the hands of a knowledgeable, clever, and most of all <i>prepared</i> Mage. The Enlightened are not ones to be fucked with idly, they're essentially Human++, and humans in general are <i>cheating bastards</i> when it comes down to the wire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6072-quantumboost","name":"Quantumboost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6072_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"590"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}